    "title": "Passwords, secrets and HTTPS",
    "body": "Passwords are stored as a **slow hash with a salt**, tuned to about 100 ms: 50 logins per second cost 5 CPU cores, but a stolen table yields only about 10 guesses per second per core. The database password lives in a **secret store**, not in code, and only the services that need it can read it. Every connection uses **HTTPS**, between internal services too, so nobody on the network can read or change requests.",
